— 🇺🇸 Acting Secretary of the Navy Hung Cao has stated to the U.S Senate that the Navy will be forced to operate with 9 of 11 legally mandated aircraft carriers for the remainder of the 2020s and potentially into the 2030s due to the consistent reshuffling of funds that Congress is doing, along with the extended delays in the Gerald R. Ford-class aircraft carrier program & general maintenance concerns.

The USS John F. Kennedy isn’t expected to replace the USS Nimitz until 2028/2029 or even 2030 given the fires that hit the USS Gerald R. Ford (as more than 1,000 crew quarters’ beds and a decent number of oddities were removed off the JFK to replace the damaged stuff on the Ford, however this merely sat them back a handful of weeks.), but consistent changing of budgets, redesigning ships and such; and a bigger issue of congress not providing the proper funding for known long-term components (such as the Ford’s Electromagnetic Aircraft Launch & Recovery System or EMALS system, a known long-lead time component for manufacturing) until the vessel NEEDS it at that time, which causes further delay; or another example of simply not providing the shipyard with the full funding of a vessel up front, and instead releasing the absolute bare minimum in funding upon the completion of a pre-agreed portion/module of a ship, which prevents shipyards from being able to hire, provide raises for shipyard workers, or really do anything except for said bare minimum.

